#lang ivy1.7
# Ivy language version 1.7 syntax (isolates, implement, etc.) is used below.

# Pulls in Ivy's standard library file order.ivy. This spec no longer instantiates
# anything from it (we state the order axioms by hand below), so this include is
# currently unused. Harmless; remove it if you want a minimal file.
#include order

# Declare epoch as an uninterpreted sort at the top level. Deliberately NO
# "interpret" here: a top-level interpret (e.g. epoch -> bv[63]) would make every
# verification condition in the file, including the whole membership protocol,
# use bit-vector arithmetic and leave EPR. The interpretation is confined to
# epocher.implementation further down.

# epocher is the foundation module. Other code should talk to epoch through it.
object epoch = {
    type this

    # Strict order on epochs, as a relation rather than the built-in <. A relation
    # is EPR-friendly, and its name does not clash with the built-in < that epoch
    # gets once it is interpreted as bv[63] in the implementation.
    relation lt(X:this, Y:this)

    # "Y is the immediate successor of X". A relation, NOT a function epoch -> epoch:
    # a function from a sort to itself creates a stratification cycle and leaves EPR.
    relation succ(X:this, Y:this)

    # The least epoch.
    individual zero : this

    # The next epoch to be handed out. This is the only mutable state.
    individual next_epoch : this

    # Declare fresh_epoch once, with no body. The spec constrains it via after/ensure
    # and the implementation supplies the only body. (Giving it a body in both places
    # was the "multiple implementations" error.)
    action fresh_epoch returns (e:this)

    # Everything in this section is what OTHER isolates (the protocol) get to see.
    # It contains no arithmetic, so their VCs stay in EPR.
    specification {

        # lt is transitive.
        axiom lt(X,Y) & lt(Y,Z) -> lt(X,Z)

        # lt is irreflexive.
        axiom ~lt(X,X)

        # lt is total: any two epochs are equal or ordered.
        axiom lt(X,Y) | X = Y | lt(Y,X)

        # zero is the minimum: nothing is below it.
        axiom ~lt(X, zero)

        # succ is the immediate successor: Y is above X with nothing strictly between.
        # All quantifiers are universal (Z is implicitly ∀), so this stays in EPR.
        # NOTE: this axiom says the maximum element has NO successor (see the note
        # on the definition of succ in the implementation section).
        axiom succ(X,Y) <-> (lt(X,Y) & ~(lt(X,Z) & lt(Z,Y)))

        # Initially the next epoch to hand out is zero.
        after init { next_epoch := zero }

        # Contract for fresh_epoch: after it runs, the returned e is the old value of
        # next_epoch and the new next_epoch is e's immediate successor. So every
        # call returns a strictly larger epoch than all earlier calls. This is the
        # only thing the protocol can rely on about fresh_epoch.
        after fresh_epoch returns (e:this) {
            ensure succ(e, next_epoch)
        }
    }

    # Only used when this isolate itself is verified or compiled. This is the ONE
    # place where bit-vector arithmetic appears.
    implementation {

        # Give epoch a concrete 63-bit unsigned machine representation. This is what
        # lets ivy_to_cpp compile it. It is scoped to this section, so the protocol
        # isolates never see it.
        interpret this -> bv[63]

        # Bridge the abstract relation to the built-in bit-vector order. With this,
        # the order axioms above are true facts about bv[63].
        definition lt(X,Y) = X < Y

        # Bridge the abstract zero to the bit-vector 0.
        # This must be a definition, not a bare declaration: a declared-but-undefined
        # relation over bv[63] becomes runtime state, and the C++ test harness tries to
        # enumerate all 2^63 values of it (the earlier compile error).
        definition zero = 0

        # succ as a definition, for the same reason as above.
        # CAUTION: as written, succ(max, 0) is TRUE (+ wraps in bv[63]), yet the axiom
        # in the specification says the maximum has no successor. If ivy_check
        # includes that axiom here, it is false in this model, which could make proofs
        # in this isolate vacuous. A safer definition is
        definition succ(X,Y) = (X < Y & Y = X + 1)
        # which agrees with the axiom at the maximum and makes the assume below
        # do real work in proving the ensure.
        # was (less safe):
        #definition succ(X,Y) = (Y = X + 1)

        # The only body of fresh_epoch. It returns the current counter and advances it.
        implement fresh_epoch  returns (e:this) {

            # TRUSTED ASSUMPTION: the counter is not at its maximum (2^63 - 1), so the
            # +1 below does not wrap around to 0.
            #
            # Why it is here: the spec promises epochs increase forever ("every epoch
            # has a successor"). A 63-bit counter cannot keep that promise, because at
            # the maximum +1 wraps to 0, which would break monotonicity. The assume
            # says that state never occurs.
            #
            # Why assume rather than require: a require is an obligation on the caller,
            # so tick and then the entire protocol would have to prove
            # next_epoch < 2^63-1, which drags bit-vector reasoning into every
            # protocol proof, the thing this file is designed to avoid.
            #
            # Trade-offs:
            #  * Soundness: it is not proved. Everything verified holds only for
            #    executions that stay below the limit. If the assumption is false,
            #    the proofs say nothing about the wrapped-around behavior.
            #  * Practicality: reaching 2^63 calls is not realistic. At a billion
            #    epochs per second it takes about 290 years.
            #  * No runtime check, as far as I know: the compiled code will not stop
            #    or warn if the limit were hit. If you want that, add an explicit
            #    check in the real deployment, outside the verified core.
            #  * Alternatives: (1) require: proved but pushes the burden onto every
            #    caller; (2) an invariant next_epoch < max: fresh_epoch cannot
            #    preserve it alone, since the increment can reach the max, so you would
            #    need a bound on the number of calls; (3) keep the assume and record
            #    it as a documented assumption of the implementation refinement.
            assume next_epoch < 9223372036854775807;   # trusted: no wraparound

            # Return the current counter as the fresh epoch.
            e := next_epoch;

            # Advance the counter by one. Real arithmetic, but only in this section.
            next_epoch := next_epoch + 1
        }
    }
}

#instance epocherTestInstance : epoch

# The environment is told about each fresh epoch through this callback. It is an
# import: something outside the verified code provides it. It is here just so
# we can observe in the terminal what the tick action below is doing.
import action report(e:epoch)

# The exported entry point that the environment (or test harness) can call.
export action tick = { 
   # Get a fresh epoch through the abstract interface. This call sees only the
   # specification of fresh_epoch, not the bv arithmetic.
   var x := epoch.fresh_epoch; 

   # Report it to the environment. (so we can see it counting up in the terminal)
   # No real implementation is needed. We just want the side effect that the
   # ivy2cpp/ivy_to_cpp/ivy2golang fuzzer prints as it works.
   call report(x) 
}
